        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Before choosing anything, know what a barn door cannot do: it does not seal, it does not lock securely, and it needs a clear run of wall as wide as the door itself. Where those things are acceptable, it is an excellent solution.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="before-you-buy" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. What a Barn Door Cannot Do
        </h2>

        <p>
          Barn doors are genuinely useful in the right opening and genuinely frustrating in the wrong one, so it is worth being honest about the limitations before spending anything.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They do not seal.</strong> A barn door hangs in front of an opening rather than sitting inside a frame, so there is a gap all the way round — typically half an inch at the sides and more at the bottom. Sound passes through it, light passes through it, and cooking smells pass through it. That is inherent to the format and no amount of money fixes it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They do not lock properly.</strong> There is no way to fit a conventional mortice lock to a door with no frame to latch into. The available hooks and latches signal privacy rather than provide security. For a bedroom, a main bathroom or anywhere a child might need to be shut out, that is a real consideration.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They need a wall.</strong> This is the constraint that most often kills the idea. The door has to go somewhere when it is open, and that somewhere is a clear stretch of wall at least as wide as the door itself. Light switches, outlets, radiators, vents, return corners and pictures all have to be absent from that run.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They need structure.</strong> A door plus hardware can easily exceed a hundred pounds, and it is a moving load. Drywall anchors will not hold it. There must be framing behind the track or a solid backer board fixed to that framing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They need headroom.</strong> The track sits above the opening, so you need several inches of clear wall between the door casing and the ceiling. Rooms with low ceilings or deep crown moulding frequently cannot take one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Where a barn door excels is a pantry, a laundry, a closet, a home office or an ensuite off a bedroom — openings where a swinging door wastes floor space and where sealing and locking are not important. Our <a href="/barn-door-vs-french-door/">barn door vs French door comparison</a> works through the alternatives in more detail.
        </p>

        <h2 id="hardware" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. The Hardware Matters More Than the Door
        </h2>

        <p>
          Most people choose the slab first and treat the track as an accessory. That is backwards. The door is a static object you look at; the hardware is a mechanism you operate several times a day, and it is where cheap products announce themselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Rollers</strong> are the single most important component. Sealed bearings run quietly and keep running quietly; unsealed ones collect dust and start to grind within months. Nylon-covered wheels are quieter than bare steel on a steel track and are worth seeking out.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Track gauge</strong> is the second. Quality tracks are solid, heavy steel bar. Budget ones are thin folded stock that flexes under load, and flex is what makes a door judder rather than glide. Pick the track up if you can — weight is a reliable proxy for quality here.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Weight rating</strong> should be read with scepticism. The published figure is a static rating, and a moving door generates dynamic forces well above its own weight, particularly when it hits the end stop. Choose hardware rated comfortably above your slab rather than exactly at it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Anti-jump discs</strong> stop the door lifting off the track if it is pushed hard or catches on something. They are inexpensive, they are a genuine safety component, and the cheapest kits omit them.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The floor guide</strong> keeps the bottom of the door against the wall. Without it the slab swings out at the base, bangs the architrave and loads the rollers sideways. Never fit a barn door without one.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Soft-close dampers</strong> catch the door in the final inches and prevent it slamming. On a heavy slab this is the difference between a door you enjoy using and one that makes the whole house flinch.
        </p>


        <!-- Product Reviews -->
