        <h2 id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Sizing and Wall Space
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sizing a barn door is not the same as sizing a conventional one, and getting it wrong is expensive because slabs are rarely returnable once finished.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Width:</strong> the door should be two to three inches wider than the opening on <em>each</em> side. For a standard 32 inch doorway that means a slab of 36 to 38 inches. The overlap is what actually covers the opening and limits the gap that light and sound pass through.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Height:</strong> two to three inches taller than the opening, and then check the total stack. Door height plus roller hardware plus track has to fit between the top of the casing and the ceiling. This is where low ceilings and deep crown moulding cause problems, and it is worth measuring before ordering anything.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Track length:</strong> twice the width of the door. The track must extend a full door width beyond the opening so the slab can clear it completely. A 36 inch door needs roughly a 72 inch track, which is why the common kit lengths are what they are.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Wall run:</strong> a clear stretch at least as wide as the door, on the side it slides towards. Walk that wall before buying and look for switches, sockets, thermostats, radiators, air vents, return corners and anything hanging on it. Every one of those is a problem, and a light switch that ends up behind an open door is a daily annoyance rather than a minor one.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Clearance in front:</strong> the door hangs roughly an inch off the wall, plus the thickness of the slab. Check that this does not foul the casing of an adjacent door, a skirting board return or a piece of furniture.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single Door or a Pair?</h3>

        <p>
          For openings wider than about four feet, two doors meeting in the middle are usually better than one large slab. A single wide door needs an enormous clear wall run to slide into, weighs a great deal, and gathers real momentum when pushed. A pair splits the weight, halves the wall space needed on each side, and looks more deliberate on a wide opening such as a living room or a large closet.
        </p>

        <p>
          The trade-off is cost and complexity: two slabs, a longer track, and twice the hardware, plus the need to get both doors hanging at exactly the same height so they meet cleanly in the middle. Bypass kits, where two doors run on parallel tracks and pass one another, are a third option worth knowing about for closets where there is no wall to slide onto at all.
        </p>

        <h2 id="mounting" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. The Mounting Problem
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the part that catches people out, and it is worth understanding before the door arrives rather than after.
        </p>

        <p>
          A barn door track carries a heavy, moving load, and that load has to reach the building's structure. Drywall anchors — of any type, including the heavy-duty ones — are not adequate. They will hold for a while and then fail, usually all at once, and usually with the door attached.
        </p>

        <p>
          There are two acceptable approaches.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Fix directly into studs.</strong> This is fine in principle and awkward in practice, because studs sit at 16 or 24 inch centres and track mounting holes rarely align with them. Some tracks have slotted or repositionable mounts that help. Locating the framing accurately is essential — a <a href="/best-stud-finder/">good stud finder</a> earns its price on this job alone.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Fit a backer board.</strong> The more reliable method and the one most installers use. A length of 1x6 or similar is fixed across several studs above the opening, and the track is mounted to that. It spreads the load properly, gives you complete freedom on fixing positions, and can be finished to look like an intentional architectural detail. It also raises the track off the wall by its thickness, which needs allowing for in the height calculation.
        </p>

        <p>
          One further note: the header above a door opening is usually solid framing, but the wall either side of it may not be, and the track extends well past the opening. Do not assume that because you found timber above the door you will find it along the whole track run.
        </p>

        <h2 id="installing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Installation Notes
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Finish the door before hanging it.</strong> Stain or paint all faces and every edge, including the top and bottom, before it goes up. Sealing all sides evenly is what keeps a solid wood slab from cupping as humidity changes, and doing it on trestles is far easier than doing it on a hanging door.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Get the track dead level.</strong> A barn door will roll towards the low end of a track that is even slightly out, which means it drifts open on its own. Use a long spirit level or a laser rather than a short torpedo level, and check it again after fixing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Work out your height carefully.</strong> Track height is set by the door height plus the hardware, and the finished slab should clear the floor by about half an inch — enough for carpet or a rug, not so much that the gap is obvious. Dry-fit and measure before drilling.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Fit the anti-jump discs and the floor guide.</strong> Neither is optional. The discs prevent the door lifting off the track, and the guide keeps the base against the wall. Skipping them is how a door ends up damaging its own trim.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Get help with the lift.</strong> A solid or glass slab is heavy and awkward, and it has to be lifted above head height and located onto rollers. This is genuinely a two-person job, and attempting it alone risks both the door and the installer.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Adjust and then check again in a month.</strong> Most kits allow roller height adjustment; use it to fine-tune the gap along the bottom. Then recheck the fixings after a few weeks of use, because the settling-in period is when an underspecified fixing will start to loosen — far better to find that with a spanner than the hard way.
        </p>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Ordering a door the same size as the opening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A barn door must overlap the opening by two to three inches on each side and above, or it will not cover it. Ordering to the opening dimension is the most common and most expensive sizing error.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Forgetting the wall space beside the opening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The door has to go somewhere when open. You need clear wall at least as wide as the door — no switches, outlets, radiators, artwork or return walls in that run.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Mounting the track into drywall alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A door and hardware can weigh well over a hundred pounds and it moves. Anchors in drywall will fail, usually suddenly. The track needs studs or a properly fixed backer board.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using a barn door on a bathroom without thinking it through</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">There is a visible gap all round, so it does not block sound or light and it does not lock like a proper door. Fine for an ensuite, questionable for a main bathroom in a shared house.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying a kit rated at exactly the door's weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Ratings are for static load. A moving door generates dynamic forces well above its weight. Choose hardware rated comfortably above your slab, not exactly at it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How much wider should a barn door be than the opening?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Two to three inches wider on each side and two to three inches taller at the top. That overlap is what actually covers the opening and limits the light gap. For a standard 32 inch doorway, a 36 or 38 inch door slab is the usual answer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How much wall space does a barn door need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">At least the full width of the door, clear of obstructions, on the side it slides to. That wall must have no light switches, outlets, radiators, vents or return corners in the path, and no artwork you are unwilling to move.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do barn doors lock?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Not securely. Various latches and hooks exist and they provide privacy signalling rather than security — a barn door cannot take a conventional mortice lock and there is always a gap around the slab. For a bedroom or main bathroom that matters.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a barn door on a bathroom?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">You can, and many people do, but understand the compromise. The gap around the slab means sound and light pass through, and the latch options are limited. It works well for an ensuite off a bedroom and less well for a shared family bathroom.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight can barn door hardware hold?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Consumer kits are typically rated between 200 and 400 lbs static. Choose hardware rated well above your door's actual weight, because a moving door generates dynamic loads considerably higher than its static weight.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need a bottom guide?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. Without one the door swings away from the wall at the bottom, bangs the trim and eventually damages the rollers. A floor guide or a wall-mounted bottom guide is not optional, and most kits include one.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can barn doors be installed on drywall?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Only into the framing behind it. Fixing a track into drywall anchors alone will fail. Either land every fixing in a stud, or mount a solid backer board across the studs first and fix the track to that.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a soft-close mechanism worth it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">On a heavy door, yes. It stops the slab slamming into the end stop, which is both loud and hard on the hardware over time. On a light hollow-core door it is a nice-to-have rather than a necessity.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Related Interior Door Guides
